ple would read that was literate and  then it was engaging for the customer  and so what we did is we featured I  think of the first season five products  and we talked about the greenhouse gas  emissions generated that the energy used  the waste generated and eventually went  to water but we also we also put in a  narrative about okay this is what we  like about what we do with this product  we use recycled polyester we use organic  cotton this is what we think is still a  problem and this is what we think we'll  do next so the idea was really to talk  to consumers and in a sotto voce way to  NGOs who were asking these questions but  the unintended consequences were there  are a couple of interesting things first  I had been with the company more or less  since the beginning and I noticed after  footprint  calls that we had a great reduction in  the number of hostile water-cooler  conversations that come about when  people talk about what we're trying to  do environmentally and how irrelevant it  is to the business there was still that  kind of much more that kind of tension  in the company before footprint  chronicles when we had actually  explained to our own people a lot of  what we were doing and we actually  elevated the conversation so that you  still have the bickering back and forth  but the bickering is smarter and then  the the second unintended consequence we  noticed that our production people who  had been kind of nervous when we were  you know putting up the factories on the  website and talking about what we liked  and didn't like about what they were  responsible for that they started to  come to us because they found the  footprint chronicles a tool for working  with the suppliers to improve practices  another unintended consequence that we  found out that suppliers wanted to be  mentioned on the footprint chronicles  even if they were mentioned critically  because it gave them some face to the  consumer and faced with other businesses  so what we 