n that  responsibility and they felt empowered  when they left to take that part of  their work into the other parts of their  job and we really saw that flourish  which was really exciting to watch and  it was a really great reminder how  important it is to spread this  responsibility out into the organization  so you have people on different places  working towards the same goal and  actually moved out it was contagious so  we had the team that was responsible for  creating the footprint chronicles but  then once we started to engage the  production staff more heavily they began  to behave just like the team that was  working on the Chronicles so they were  tremendously self directed and  self-motivated to get things done now  sometimes I am teaching a course this  semester called corporate environmental  management and strategy and sometimes  there are very difficult trade-offs as  as you progress you know there might be  a technical product requirements  and you really need to use something  even if it isn't the most environmental  product can you can you give us an  example of of a challenge like that  where you really had to make some kind  of a difficult choice in that way yeah  there's a current one that we're in the  middle of actually and that's around  performance characteristics in Alpine  and ski gear specifically around  waterproofing and trying to balance what  is the performance requirement of a  product in the scheme of the durability  over a lifetime and the performance and  environmental ramifications of some of  the chemicals that are needed to do that  so in the our case it's around durable  water repellent finishes which is what  you apply to the outside so you stay dry  in a rainstorm and we have reported as  Vincent said on the footprint chronicles  about the use of this chemistry and  trying to figure out how to reduce the  environmental impacts yet maintain a  level of performance that still keeps  the product competitive in the market  and