e number of  sales we might get but improving the  loyalty of the customers we have and  gaining new customers who hadn't bought  from us before yes  my name is Clara I'm a first year  masters of Environmental Management  student here at FES my question is about  how you've seen product prices and  potentially consumer demographics change  with your changing aspiration and  whether or not you think that  potentially reinforces ideas that  sustainability and responsibility are  sort of luxury ideas well I think with  more companies engaging in it it's  easier because there's an economy of  scale that develops and that is a huge  part of our business is economy of scale  and part of our sharing of our  information with our competitors  sometimes is a business actually have  set up a little booth at a trade show  ourselves saying please come use some of  this wool because we know we can't  sustain the goals that are there so  economies of scale help a lot I think  also in the years we've been doing this  now we've seen kind of in the early  years all the green consumers yes we'll  pay more and then you put it to the  general public and that doesn't really  happen but I think now we're seeing a  shift that people care more about the  origin of their products they care more  about where things are coming from I've  said to a few people here it's always a  great reminder to be on universities and  seeing the different generations where  the upcoming ones are used to this  transparent lifestyle and it's not only  comfortable but it's expected where is  even in where I said it's still a little  like really you want to know what that  was on Facebook I mean and so it you  know it's I think there's this shift and  and with that shift people understand  more of what's happening which hopefully  allows them to understand the value of  the pricing that exists on it and the  footprint chronicles was a big big help  in that because you know I think when  marketing is really good r